/Type /StructElem
/S /H1
/P 54 0 R
/T (This project showed how the lab topics fit together in one design: references and const make C++ code safer than C; overloading and default arguments reduce the number of function names; encapsulation protects data; constructors, destructors and the copy constructor manage dynamic memory; static members hold class- wide data; friend functions give controlled access; and operator overloading lets objects behave like built-in types. An issue resolved: with the default shallow copy, two accounts would point to the same name array and delete it twice, so a deep-copy copy constructor was written. Another point was that operator+ must return a new Account by value, so its temporary object is created and destroyed correctly.)
/E (This project showed how the lab topics fit together in one design: references and const make C++ code safer than C; overloading and default arguments reduce the number of function names; encapsulation protects data; constructors, destructors and the copy constructor manage dynamic memory; static members hold class- wide data; friend functions give controlled access; and operator overloading lets objects behave like built-in types. An issue resolved: with the default shallow copy, two accounts would point to the same name array and delete it twice, so a deep-copy copy constructor was written. Another point was that operator+ must return a new Account by value, so its temporary object is created and destroyed correctly.)
/Pg 16 0 R
/K [5]
